  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\n...TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY NIGHT...\n\nEarly this morning, upper air analysis and water vapor satellite \nimagery shows a sharp, postively-tilted trough over the Ohio \nRiver Valley and the Great Lakes region. Meanwhile, a strong \nmid/upper ridge remains stretched out from the Desert Southwest \nto the central Rockies. This ridge will gradually slide over the\ncentral plains over the next couple of days supporting a \nwarming trend across the forecast area. For today, highs and \nlows will be near average for this time of year. However, \nseasonably hot conditions will return for Monday and Tuesday as \nthe ridge axis slides overhead. While afternoon highs Monday and\nTuesday will be in the mid 90s to near 100, dewpoints in the \n50s and 60s should keep heat indices near the ambient \ntemperature. Additionally, lows in the upper 60s to low 70s \nshould provide some relief during the overnight period, which \nruns in the contrast to the heat stress the area experienced \nlast week. To summarize, despite temperatures being slightly \nabove average, this period of warmer temperatures will not feel \nquite as hot as the previous period of hot weather.\n\n...WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY...\n\nBy mid week, mid range deterministic and ensemble model guidance is \nhoning in on fairly robust deep layer troughing across the northern \nCONUS. This should shove mid/upper ridging across the southern and \nsouthwestern CONUS and open the door for zonal upper flow to return \nover the central plains. A series of embedded impulses within this \nupper flow will traverse over the region bringing the potential for \nmultiple opportunities at rainfall across the area. Details continue \nto remain a little fuzzy as it will likely come down to specific \nmesoscale features that determine exactly who see rainfall and \nhow much rainfall occurs as any given location. That being said,\nrain chances are increasing slightly across the area from \nWednesday morning through Friday night. Temperature-wise, \nafternoon highs and overnight lows are forecast to remain \nslightly above average for the beginning of August.",
